-  sept [order=10]
-  shrine_types [order=20]
-  bond [order=25]
-  shrine [order=30]
'  Lain's shared protocol: records, commands, outcomes, slot vocabulary,
'  and constructors. No namespace reads or event execution.
+  vine
  :  u={\ task nat nat}sov
+  dep
  '  One cared path: a (care, path) pair.  News carries them as
  '  subscriptions (p is the subscriber); hear arms receive them as
  '  changes (p is the changed path).
  :  c=care p=path
+  kook
  '  The value of the well-known behavior slot: a list of form-node
  '  refs — namespace paths, each read directly at resolution.  Any
  '  node holding a form at ['form] is thereby a registry entry, so
  '  behaviors are rerootable; the kernel's own live under /kook by
  '  convention (kook_root).
  :  refs=row[path]
+  crew
  '  A node's dependencies: a map from SLOT to (care, path) dep.
  '  The key is purely indexical — a local name, exactly like a
  '  myth's slot keys — so behaviors find their views by name and
  '  the watched paths can move without them noticing.
  mop[path dep lt_pith]
  +  sing
    '  A crew of one: a single named dependency.
    \  slot=path d=dep
    ^  crew
    (mop/put[path dep lt_pith] slot d (mop/empty))
+  cpail
  '  The value of the well-known /sys/crew slot.
  :  =crew
+  pails
  '  The simple pail wrappers: one payload each, distinguished only
  '  by their brand — the brand IS the meaning, so hosts and the
  '  explorer unwrap by rep.
  +  n
    '  A nat payload, rendered as decimal.
    :  n=nat
  +  t
    '  A text cord.  The explorer renders it verbatim (a pails/n
    '  would print as decimal digits).  The /boot mirror uses it
    '  for source text, entry kinds, and documentation.
    :  t=str
  +  q
    '  A iota payload — behavior ids riding as slot values.
    :  q=iota
  +  p
    '  A path payload — request targets riding as slot values.
    :  p=path
  +  code
    '  A compiled entry riding opaquely in the namespace: the /boot
    '  mirror stores each declaration's compiled value here.  The
    '  any type is the untyped carrier (the value may be a law, row, or pin) —
    '  read it back out, never do arithmetic on it.
    :  v=any
  +  ty
    '  A reflected type descriptor (the builtin `type` sum) — the
    '  /boot mirror stores each entry's type here and the explorer
    '  pretty-prints it (tyshow renders, not baked text, so the
    '  printer can improve without reseeding the mirror).
    :  t=type
  +  ps
    '  A row-of-paths payload — the /boot mirror's reference lists
    '  riding as slot values.
    :  ps=row[path]
+  pd
  '  A data pail.
  \  x=nat
  ^  pail
  (pails/n x)
+  pt
  '  A text pail: pd's twin, for hosts that hold a cord rather than a
  '  number.  Both exist because pails/n and pails/t are nominal
  '  records — a host reaching for /pails/t through the subject gets
  '  the TYPE, not a constructor, and silently builds an unreadable
  '  slot (which is how the chat app's credential became an empty
  '  Bearer header).  Call these, not the record paths.
  \  x=str
  ^  pail
  (pails/t x)
+  twig
  '  Limb constructors for the common wrappers: one idiom for every
  '  slot creation — t.add((twig/nat ['n] 7)).
  +  nat
    \  s=path n=nat
    ^  twig
    (twig s (pails/n n))
  +  txt
    \  s=path t=str
    ^  twig
    (twig s (pails/t t))
  +  iota
    \  s=path q=iota
    ^  twig
    (twig s (pails/q q))
  +  pat
    \  s=path p=path
    ^  twig
    (twig s (pails/p p))
+  myth
  '  Slot readers, unwrapping the common pails by rep.
  +  nat_at
    '  Read a data slot, with default.
    \  slot=path d=nat t=myth
    ^  nat
    ?  t.get(slot)
     > .none  d
     > (./some pl=pail)
       ?  pl
        > x=pails/n  x.n
       d
  +  txt_at
    '  Read a text slot, or the empty string when absent or foreign.
    \ slot=path t=myth
    ^ str
    > (./some held) t.get(slot)
      ""
    > text=pails/t held
      ""
    text.t
  +  code_at
    '  Read a compiled-value slot: the code pail's payload at slot,
    '  or 0.
    \  slot=path t=myth
    ^  any
    ?  t.get(slot)
     > .none  0
     > (./some pl=pail)
       ?  pl
        > x=pails/code  x.v
       0
  +  quip_at
    '  Read a iota slot: the pails/q payload at slot, if any.
    \  slot=path t=myth
    ^  maybe[iota]
    ?  t.get(slot)
     > .none  .none
     > (./some pl=pail)
       ?  pl
        > x=pails/q  (./some x.q)
       .none
  +  pail_at
    '  Read a slot's raw pail, or a zero pails/n when absent —
    '  host-side consumers unwrap by rep (the brand cord rides in
    '  the head).
    \  slot=path t=myth
    ^  pail
    ?  t.get(slot)
     > (./some pl=pail)  pl
     > .none  (pd 0)
  +  has_at
    '  Whether the myth has anything at slot — a monomorphic wrapper
    '  over the mop method, so hosts can call it across the FFI.
    \  slot=path t=myth
    ^  bool
    t.has(slot)
+  mode
  '  What kind of change happened at a path.
  &  mode/add
     mode/dif
     mode/del

  +  add
    :

  +  dif
    :

  +  del
    :
+  writ
  '  An incoming command: replace the myth, merge into it, delete the
  '  node — or WARP it: apply a declared operation to the current
  '  record, the input riding the event and never the namespace.
  &  writ/make
     writ/poke
     writ/cull
     writ/warp

  +  warp
    '  Poke-with-an-overlay: op names the op card (its ['sys 'op]
    '  slot holds the op record, fn inside), input is the transient
    '  argument record.  Dispatch applies (fn input cur) and commits
    '  the result as a REPLACE — the fn computed the whole new
    '  record, so slots it dropped vanish (a meld would resurrect
    '  them).  The commit gate judges the final record as for any
    '  write.
    :  op=path input=myth

  +  make
    :  =myth

  +  poke
    :  =myth

  +  cull
    :
+  move
  '  An outbound effect: one command, emitted at src, addressed
  '  outside the processed subtree.
  :  src=path dst=path w=writ
+  job
  '  What a dispatch sees: a command (the target is the flue's dst),
  '  a child-change gift, or a dependency-change notification of
  '  (care, path) pairs.
  &  job/vine
     job/take
     job/hear

  +  vine
    :  w=writ

  +  take
    :  gift=axal[mode]

  +  hear
    :  changed=row[dep]
+  flue
  '  A linearized work unit: who caused it, where it lands, what it is.
  :  src=path dst=path job=job
+  bowl
  '  Per-activation context.  now is a logical clock, +1 per
  '  activation within the event; kids is the in-flight care-y view
  '  of the node's own subtree; crew holds the hydrated dependency
  '  views the node declared in its /sys/crew slot, keyed by their
  '  indexical slot names.
  :  src=path here=path now=nat eny=nat kids=axal[myth] crew=mop[path axal[myth] lt_pith]
+  yell
  '  What an arm returns: cards (path-tagged writs) to emit and the
  '  node's new state.
  :  cards=row[[p=path q=writ]] out=myth
+  form
  '  A behavior: six laws over (bowl, state, input).  dead and goof
  '  are vocabulary only in this port — never invoked.
  :  init={\ bowl myth}yell
     talk={\ bowl myth myth}yell
     take={\ bowl myth axal[mode]}yell
     hear={\ bowl myth row[dep]}yell
     dead={\ bowl myth}yell
     goof={\ bowl myth}yell
+  def_init
  \  b=bowl t=myth
  ^  yell
  (yell [] t)
+  def_talk
  '  Default talk: union, the poke wins.
  \  b=bowl cur=myth add=myth
  ^  yell
  =  o cur.meld(add)
  (yell [] o)
+  def_take
  \  b=bowl cur=myth g=axal[mode]
  ^  yell
  (yell [] cur)
+  def_hear
  \  b=bowl cur=myth r=row[dep]
  ^  yell
  (yell [] cur)
+  def_dead
  \  b=bowl t=myth
  ^  yell
  (yell [] t)
+  def_form
  '  The default behavior: init/talk as documented, take/hear keep
  '  state.
  ^  form
  (form def_init def_talk def_take def_hear def_dead def_dead)
+  kres
  '  Kook resolution: the behavior list, or a failure code.
  &  kres/kf
     kres/ke

  +  kf
    :  forms=row[form]

  +  ke
    :  err=iota
+  task
  '  One top-level event: a single path-tagged command, or change
  '  notifications (changed path -> its cared subscribers, as /dep
  '  pairs).
  &  task/vine
     task/hear

  +  vine
    :  p=path w=writ

  +  hear
    :  news=axal[row[dep]]
+  fate
  '  The kernel's answer: resolved notes in /lte_pith (tap) order
  '  and outbound commands. Warp can occur only in commands, not notes.
  :  write=row[[p=path q=note]] effects=row[move]
+  worm
  '  The per-event transaction.  down is a row+cursor queue (pop at
  '  dcur, push snoc).  prize records how each path changed, for the
  '  gift cascade.
  :  toil=axal[note] down=row[flue] dcur=nat up=row[move] prize=axal[mode] now=nat eny=nat
+  wend
  '  The loop accumulator: a live transaction or an aborted one.
  &  wend/go
     wend/bad

  +  go
    :  =worm

  +  bad
    :  err=iota
+  doom
  '  The kernel outcome for one event.  A successful vine event acks
  '  with the command's path tag; hear events ack .none.
  &  doom/done
     doom/fail

  +  done
    :  =fate ack=maybe[path]

  +  fail
    :  err=iota
+  ovum
  '  The full-cycle outcome: new sovereign, outbound moves, the step's
  '  change signs, and the command's ack (its path tag; .none for hear
  '  events) — or a failure with the sovereign untouched.
  &  ovum/ripe
     ovum/fail

  +  ripe
    :  state=sov effects=row[move] signs=axal[sign] ack=maybe[path]

  +  fail
    :  err=iota
+  limb_slot
  '  The well-known slot holding a node's behavior id list
  '  (bond/limb: the behavior chain).
  ^  path
  bond/limb
+  crew_slot
  '  The well-known slot holding a node's dependency map
  '  (bond/crew).
  ^  path
  bond/crew
+  is_xeno
  '  Is this path remote (under /xeno)?
  \ p=path
  ^ bool
  p.starts('xeno)

+  is_io
  '  Is this path under the driver boundary (/io)?  Crew-derived
  '  subscriptions skip such nodes: their reactivity belongs to the
  '  driver through persistent watches, not kernel hears.
  \ p=path
  ^ bool
  p.starts('io)

+  is_h
  '  Is this path under the temporal-transposition overlay (/h)?
  '  Read-only and derived: writes here fail the event.
  \ p=path
  ^ bool
  p.starts('h)

+  is_o
  '  Is this path under the overlay lens (/o)?  Read-only and
  '  derived: writes here fail the event.
  \ p=path
  ^ bool
  p.starts('o)

+  is_xv
  '  Is this path under the view lens (/x)?  /x/...path runs the
  '  pinned weft and tack selected by the defining datum's case.  Read-only and derived, like /h and /o.
  \ p=path
  ^ bool
  p.starts('x)

+  log_root
  '  The event journal: one node per accepted post-boot epoch,
  '  /log/<epoch>, holding the driving task, its ack, and its
  '  outbound effects.  Commit-owned — kernel writes here fail the
  '  event, and crew subscriptions to /log are skipped, so the
  '  journal never feeds back into reactivity.  Signs for /log
  '  paths still mint but are inert (no eyes ever match them).
  ^  path
  ['log]
+  is_log
  '  Is this path under the event journal (/log)?
  \ p=path
  ^ bool
  p.starts('log)

+  log_card
  '  One journal entry: the input event verbatim, the ack it earned,
  '  and the outbound moves it produced.  Rides as the ['ev] slot of
  '  the /log/<epoch> node, committed in the event's own step.
  :  t=task ack=maybe[path] fx=row[move]
+  kook_root
  '  Where the KERNEL's behaviors live, by convention only — kook
  '  refs are full paths and resolve anywhere; this constant is where
  '  boot seeding puts the kernel's own form nodes.
  ^  path
  ['kook]
+  kooked
  '  An empty myth carrying kook refs — full form-node paths.
  \  refs=row[path]
  ^  myth
  =  t  myth/new
  t.put(limb_slot (kook refs))
+  eyes
  '  The watcher index: watched path -> its cared watchers, in the
  '  same dep orientation as news (p is the watcher).
  axal[row[dep]]
+  hive
  '  The crew index: what each crew-bearing node WATCHES, keyed by
  '  that node's path — /eyes turned around.  The watcher index is a
  '  projection of it (/eyes_from), so keeping THIS across an event —
  '  bumping it from each round's signs (/hive_bump) rather than
  '  re-deriving it from the whole namespace — is what makes a
  '  reactive round cost the crews rather than the namespace.  It
  '  shares /eyes' representation deliberately: a fresh axal
  '  instantiation would be a fresh monomorphization to compile.
  axal[row[dep]]
+  lens
  '  The derived-watcher index: TRIGGER path -> lens eyes.  Each
  '  entry keeps the trigger care to test against a sign, and the
  '  watcher with its original derived dep, so the news speaks the
  '  subscriber's vocabulary (rely paths are the derived paths they
  '  named in their crew).
  axal[row[[tc=care od=dep w=path]]]
+  gyre
  '  The reactive-cycle outcome: the final sovereign, all outbound
  '  moves, each round's signs (first = the driving event), and the
  '  driving event's ack — or a failure with the sovereign untouched.
  '  spill carries cycle_exhausted or a cascaded hear failure when it stopped
  '  the cascade: the committed rounds stand, but the error is
  '  SURFACED, not swallowed — the driver decides how to report it.
  &  gyre/spun
     gyre/fail

  +  spun
    :  state=sov moves=row[move] rounds=row[axal[sign]] ack=maybe[path] spill=maybe[iota]

  +  fail
    :  err=iota
